In article <2464@wheaton.UUCP> water@wheaton.UUCP (Paul Waterman) writes:
>In article <NN86302.91Jan10152046@kaarne.tut.fi> nn86302@tut.fi (Niilo Neuvo) writes:
>>Yes if your AC is below zero, then your AC will be anything between your AC
>>and zero. This determined randomly after each hit (I assume).
>
>Absolutely correct.  If your AC is below zero, the code will actually use
>an AC that is a random AC between zero and your listed AC.  This can be
>found in the mattacku() function in src/mhitu.c for those of you who are
>interested.  Also, though, if you have an AC of -n, the damage that a
>monster does to you will be reduce 1-n points, so go for those negative
>AC values!

I have looked at the code and I agree with Paul. On the other hand I saw
something in the code which  I don't like. In this particular case the
following call is made (i.e. in mattacku() in src/mhitu.c):

	rnd(10-ac); /*  where ac is the 10 - current AC */

In the file src/rnd.c the heading of rnd() is:

rnd(x)		/* 1 <= rnd(x) <= x */

How can this postcondition be met when x < 1 ???
So for very small AC (e.g. AC = -10) this function doesn't even work correctly!
Maybe this explains why I always get hit so hard when having a very low AC :-(

Also rnd uses the RND macro, which is defined as:

#define RND(x)	(int)(Rand() % (long)(x))

where rand is either rand() or lrand48().

I remember a discussion in comp.lang.c that the sign of the result of the '%'
operator is *not* clearly defined and depends on the implementation/hardware.
That is for example:
 5 % (-3) = - (5 % 3) = 2  or 
 5 % (-3) = -1 
(K&R: it always true that a = (a/b)*b + a%b for b != 0)

Also in some private mail with Kent Paul Dolan (Hi Kent, how is the weather on
Xanth :-), he told me that using the '%' operator for generation random numbers
in a given range is not fair, as the smaller numbers have a (very small) greater
change to be selected than the bigger numbers. This is due to the fact that
the maximum representable integer cannot be evenly divided by the upper bound
of the wanted range.
